  },
  "question": {
    "text": "An alternating emf <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>440</mn><mi>sin</mi><mn>100</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><mi>t</mi></math> is applied&nbsp;to a circuit containing an inductance of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mi>&#960;</mi></mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></math>. If an a.c. ammeter is connected in the circuit, its reading will be :"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>55</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>11</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "C"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>Given that&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>440</mn><mi>sin</mi><mn>100</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mi>&#960;</mi></mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></math></p><p>Angular frequency of the source is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#969;</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>rad</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>.</p><p>Now the reactance of the inductor will be,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>&#969;</mi><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><mfrac><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mi>&#960;</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi></math></p><p>Therefore, the peak current <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>440</mn><mrow><mn>100</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi></math> ammeter reads RMS value therefore reading will be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>rms</mi><mo>&#160;</mo></mrow></msub></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>rms</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math></p></blockquote>"
  }
}